#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069563} :activity/id #uuid "1f8c9db7-3784-53ad-acdf-c7009ab4e43d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050635 :topic/id #uuid "5a901d31-08d1-5602-a6b3-9ae8fa80845b" :topic/math-academy-id 418} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070020 :tutorial/content "The **point-slope form** of a line that passes through any point $(x_1,y_1)$ with slope $m$ is given by \n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m(x-x_1).\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, consider the straight line graph which passes through the points $(3,1)$ and $(5,5)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7838ffcb8f56ebffd384763ff761990e15031bdc5cdf3f0cc1f964ed69a4806.png)\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,1),$ so we can use $(x_1,y_1) = (3,1)$ in our point-slope formula. All that remains is to compute $m,$ and we can do this using the given points.\n\nFirst, calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{5-1}{5-3}=\\frac{4}{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=2$ and the point $(x_1,y_1) = (3,1)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =2(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** If we simplify this point-slope formula, we obtain the slope-intercept form of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =2(x-3) \\\\ y-1 & =2x-6 \\\\ y & =2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We could put $(x_1,y_1)=(5,5).$ In this case, the point-slope form will be different:\n\n$$\ny-5=2(x-5)\n$$\n\n \nBut the resulting simplified slope-intercept form will be the same:\n\n$$\ny = 2x-5\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "43f9e39e-fc27-549d-8ec4-b2bf4f375a33" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8695 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "866e675a-4d40-5d4f-ab69-7850b06a40d3" :step/math-academy-id 114979 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069564 :step/content {: